# modules.cpp shell specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies the target-safe mm.shell foundation, its core API, and
its caller-owned storage rules.

# Architectural boundaries

mm.shell is the target-safe, allocation-free level-1 foundation. It has no
dependencies on filesystem, environment, process management, dynamic heap
allocation, or platform-specific drivers. It builds for both host and embedded
targets.

Legacy POSIX shell services reside in mm.shell.posix and are consumed by
tools/shell. There is no dependency path from mm.shell to mm.shell.posix.

# Storage ownership rules

All persistent and scratch memory is owned by the application or caller and
passed into mm.shell as explicit spans:

- mm.shell performs no dynamic allocations (no malloc, new, or STL containers).
- Storage capacities are caller-bounded and declared in application arrays.
- Handlers preflight transaction scratch capacity before performing side effects
  or writing output. An insufficient scratch buffer returns Status::Overflow
  with no partial output and no hardware side effect.
- Registry descriptors are stored in caller-provided spans. install_pack
  preflights all entries, performing atomic all-or-none registration.

## Foundation acceptance storage profile

The Change Set 3 shell-smoke application instantiates the storage classes that
exist in the foundation API:

- source bytes: 512
- expansion scratch bytes: 512
- custom-command slots: 8
- command-substitution capture bytes: 256
- staged ordinary-output bytes: 256
- staged diagnostic-output bytes: 256
- MCU transaction scratch bytes: 256

The parser now has caller-owned token, fragment, node, link, and context spans,
but shell-smoke has not yet adopted a reviewed parser allocation profile.
Evaluator, session, and staged-output storage now have explicit caller-owned
APIs; installed-script and console storage join this profile when the
corresponding API is implemented. Variable and function storage
now have explicit caller-owned APIs; shell-smoke has not adopted their final
acceptance capacities.

# Foundation API

## Status and flow

- Status: classifies internal operation outcomes (Ok, BadArgument, Duplicate,
  NotFound, Unavailable, Unsupported, Overflow, WriteError, ReadError).
- CommandStatus: standard exit codes (Ok = 0, Failure = 1, Usage = 2,
  Unavailable = 125, CannotExecute = 126, NotFound = 127).
- Flow: execution control flow (Normal, Exit, Break, Continue, Return, Replace,
  Yield).
- CommandResult: combines Flow, an integer exit status, a typed Status error,
  and OverflowInfo identifying the exhausted storage class and required count.

## Capabilities

Capability flags govern command availability and runtime permissions.
CapabilitySet represents active or required capabilities as a bitset with no
heap allocation:

- Level selects the BareMetal, Mcu, or Posix software composition.
- ScriptProfile independently selects Embedded or Full language semantics.

- level1: core scripts, custom commands, variables, functions, substitution.
- level2: mandatory Level 1 software capabilities. Peripheral drivers (board,
  gpio, spi, i2c, uart, timer, adc, pwm, console) are optional and
  provider-derived, merged dynamically from mm.mcu.
- level3: Level 2 software capabilities plus host services (pathname, files,
  processes, pipelines, redirections, signals, environment). Level 3 does
  not falsely claim MCU hardware capabilities.

## I/O services

IoServices provides out and err ByteSink channels. ByteSink encapsulates a void*
context pointer and write and flush function pointers returning SinkResult.
MemorySink provides an in-memory buffer adapter that safely rejects writes
exceeding capacity without partial stream corruption. Its failure query returns
Status::Overflow, the configured StorageClass, and the required byte count.
DiscardSink provides an explicit sink that accepts all writes without retaining
them.

## Registry and dispatch

Registry maintains a linear table of CommandDescriptor records in caller-owned
storage. install enforces uniqueness, non-SpecialBuiltin classification, and
valid names. install_pack ensures atomic preflight validation before mutation.
Only an internal mm.shell partition can atomically install the standard pack
that owns SpecialBuiltin descriptors. Application code has no such entry point.
The exact name `[` is admitted for the required embedded test builtin; brackets
inside other descriptor names remain invalid. dispatch verifies nonempty argv
matching the descriptor name and required capabilities before invoking the
handler.

## Bounded word expansion in progress

expand_word consumes scanner fragments and caller-owned piece, generated-text,
field, and optional shadow-variable spans. It resolves scalar parameters,
positionals, checked arithmetic, and operands of `:-`, `:+`, and `:=`.
Nested scalar parameters are evaluated recursively with a fixed depth
bound. Assignment uses a variable fork capped at the parent's capacities;
state is committed only after field output succeeds. Failure leaves both
state and published fields unchanged. Argument-vector forms inside operands
and command substitution currently return Status::Unsupported. They are not
yet Level 1 conformance claims.

expand_value is the same expansion without field splitting, for assignment
values and case selectors. Its value_offset argument drops an unquoted
literal prefix such as NAME=, so no separate fragment copy is needed. Only a
Literal fragment may straddle that boundary; anything else is BadArgument.
The result publishes one field or none, and more than one field is
Status::Unsupported at this level.

## Function storage in progress

FunctionLibrary owns caller-supplied slots, copied source bytes, and monotonic
token, fragment, node, and link arenas. define measures and validates a body,
preflights every arena, copies the name and body source, parses into owned
storage, then publishes or replaces one visible handle. A failed definition
leaves the old handle visible. Old allocations are reclaimed on reset, not on
replacement.

A definition executed by the evaluator hands define the body's list source with
the braces stripped, not the brace group itself. The copy is the same script
either way, and a call then costs one frame instead of a redundant group
layer.

## Evaluator in progress

Evaluator begins from an EmbeddedScript and caller-owned Registry,
CommandContext, and EvaluatorStorage. EvaluatorStorage supplies expansion
scratch, argv slots and argv text, structural frames, a loop-item arena with
its text pool, case pattern bytes, the variable fork used to revert
non-persistent prefix assignments, and the staged ordinary and diagnostic
output spans. Every span is caller-owned and non-aliasing, and the evaluator
allocates nothing.

### Frames and stepping

A frame identifies a syntax index and a resumption phase, never a C++ call
frame. The frame kinds are List, AndOr, If, While, For, Case, Brace, and
Function. Each frame also names the EmbeddedScript its indices belong to,
because a function body is a separate script owned by the FunctionLibrary. Each
frame records the loop-arena marks it was pushed with, so popping it reclaims
exactly the items it added. Exhausting the frame span fails with
Status::Overflow and StorageClass::EvaluatorFrames.

step performs structural transitions or invokes one native handler, never
both. It consumes at most one work unit per structural transition and one
further unit per sixteen bytes of resumable evaluator copying: word-list
materialization for `for`, selector capture for `case`, and pattern byte
assembly. Long scans retain their cursor in the owning frame and resume on
the next call. Exhausting the budget returns Running rather than failure, and
a budget of zero makes no progress.

Argument-vector expansion for one simple command is not charged in units. Like
the work inside a native handler, it is bounded by declared capacity instead:
the caller's argv slot and argv text spans. Exceeding either is Overflow with
StorageClass::ExpandedFields or ExpandedFieldText before any dispatch, so a
maximum-capacity command cannot outrun its storage.

Four accessors make the contract testable, each describing the call that just
returned. last_step_units and last_step_handlers cover structural work: a step
never reports more units than the budget it was given and never reports two
handler entries. last_command_slots and last_command_bytes report what the last
simple command consumed of its argv capacities. Adversarial maximum-capacity
fixtures assert all four.

### Implemented productions

- lists, `&&`, `||`, and negation, including negation of a compound command;
- assignment-only commands, which expand without field splitting;
- prefix assignments, visible to the handler for that invocation only and
  reverted afterwards unless the descriptor is a SpecialBuiltin;
- `if`, `elif`, and `else`, which yield status 0 when no branch runs;
- `while`, whose status is that of the last body command, or 0 if none ran;
- `for`, over an explicit word list or, with no `in` clause, over the
  positional parameters;
- `case`, with alternative patterns and quoted metacharacters. A pattern byte
  produced by expansion is compared literally at this level;
- brace groups.

### Functions and call frames

Function definition and lookup are available only when EvaluatorStorage carries
a FunctionLibrary. Without one a definition reports Status::Unsupported and the
name never resolves.

Executing a definition rejects the name of a special builtin, then measures and
deep-copies the body into the library. Replacing a definition leaves the old
one visible until the new copy is complete.

Name resolution at a simple command is: a special builtin first, then a
function, then any other installed command, then status 127. A call is a frame,
not a handler, so it consumes no handler budget. Prefix assignments to a
function persist after it returns, exactly as they do for a special builtin.

A call frame is the body's list plus ownership of the caller's positional
parameters. ShellState::push_positionals copies the caller's slots into the
evaluator's call arena and installs the call's arguments above them in the
monotonic text pool; popping the frame restores the slots, the text cursor, and
the caller's shift count, so a callee's `$1` and its own `shift` never leak out.
The arena is bump-allocated and released in frame order.

Open calls are capped by EvaluatorStorage::call_limit. Exceeding it refuses the
call with status 2 and Overflow rather than exhausting the frame span, and the
refused call unwinds like any other failure.

### Flow ownership

A flow value is consumed only by the frame that owns it. Break and Continue
locate the innermost While or For frame, popping the frames above it; Break
pops the loop and Continue re-enters it at its next test or item. A call frame
is a barrier to both, so a body that breaks with no loop of its own does not
reach the caller's loop. Return locates the innermost call frame, which is what
restores the caller's arguments. Exit abandons every open frame and returns
Complete. Yield returns Yielded with the frame stack intact, so the next step
resumes after the yielding command.

A flow value with no owning frame leaves the stack untouched and reports status
2: Break or Continue outside a loop, and Return outside a call. Replace is
still unowned, because installed-script frames arrive with the script
library.

tested_context reports whether the command that last ran occupied a tested
context: a condition list, an inverted command, or a non-final operand of
`&&` or `||`. The flag propagates into compound commands, so the body of a
brace group used as an `if` condition is tested too.

### set -e and set -u

set -e reads that flag rather than a bare nonzero status. A command that fails
outside a tested context drops every open frame and ends the evaluator with
that status, which step reports as Complete exactly as `exit` would. A failing
condition, an inverted command, a non-final `&&` or `||` operand, and anything
inside them are exempt; a loop body is not.

set -u makes an unset parameter an error inside expansion, and that error ends
the evaluator with status 1 and Status::NotFound whether or not -e is set. A
`:-`, `:+`, or `:=` operand is exempt because the parameter is never unset by
the time it is used. A `for` word list and a `case` selector report it the same
way a simple command does, rather than as an evaluator fault. Without -u an
unset parameter still expands to nothing.

### Output staging and I/O retry

A handler never writes to a partial physical transport. The evaluator presents
an all-or-fail staging sink through the CommandContext it builds for the
dispatch, over the caller's staged_output and staged_error spans. An empty span
makes that stream write straight through, which suits a memory sink that never
blocks.

After a handler finishes, the evaluator retains its completed CommandResult and
drains the staged bytes over later step calls. Each step performs at most one
bounded drain advance, the undrained remainder of one stream. WouldBlock
consumes no byte and is retried; Failed ends the pending command with the
transport's own failure. The retained result is delivered only once its bytes
have reached the application, so step returns Running while output is pending
and no further command is selected. A partial transport write therefore never
re-enters a handler and never repeats a physical side effect. pending_output
reports that state.

Staging capacity smaller than a handler's output is an application sizing
error, not a retry case: the staging sink refuses with Overflow and
StorageClass::StagedOutput, the handler stops there, and only the bytes it had
already handed over are drained.

A handler is entered exactly once per command whatever the transport does. A
fake bounded transaction proves it: with a transport that refuses three writes
before accepting, the handler records one entry and one physical effect, and
the retried drain repeats neither. The same fake proves the scratch contract,
returning Overflow with StorageClass::TransactionScratch, no output, and no
effect when its span is too small.

Installed scripts and command substitution are not yet implemented.

## Session in progress

Session accumulates source a byte at a time into caller storage and runs each
complete command through one Evaluator. SessionStorage carries the source span,
the parser's ScriptStorage, and the EvaluatorStorage. An EmbeddedScript views
the source span, so it must outlive execution and must not alias parser or
evaluator storage.

feed distinguishes a complete command from an open one with the parser's
Incomplete result, so an `if`, loop, `case`, function, or brace group spanning
several physical lines accumulates until it closes. The states are Ready,
Continuing, Executing, and Discarding.

- a physical line ending measures the buffer. Complete parses it and starts the
  evaluator, returning the byte offset just past that terminator so the caller
  resumes the rest of its buffer after the script finishes; Incomplete keeps
  accumulating; Malformed is reported once with its location and the buffer is
  discarded unexecuted;
- an empty physical line is a no-op rather than a parse error;
- backspace and delete remove one buffered byte;
- printable bytes and horizontal tab are buffered. Tab is accepted beyond the
  recognized set of the language specification because dropping it would join
  adjacent tokens. Every other control byte is ignored;
- an overlong command reports Overflow with StorageClass::SourceBytes exactly
  once, then discards through the next physical line ending. It is never
  executed as a truncated script;
- feed is refused with Unavailable while a script is executing.

step forwards to the evaluator and reclaims the source buffer only when the
script reaches Complete or Failed, which is the point at which nothing views
those bytes any more. Session owns no history, completion, cursor movement,
ANSI handling, or transport loop: the application polls its transport and
alternates feed and step.

## Core builtins in progress

core_builtins materializes the seventeen level-1 core descriptors into
caller-owned slots: `:`, true, false, echo, printf, test, `[`, set, shift,
break, continue, return, exit, yield, capability, help, and command. It is not
an installation. install_level1 installs the complete pack atomically once
functions, installed scripts, and command substitution exist, so no
application can depend on a partial pack. A short slot span publishes nothing
and returns Overflow with StorageClass::CustomCommands.

`:`, break, continue, exit, return, set, and shift carry
CommandClass::SpecialBuiltin, which is what prefix-assignment persistence and
name resolution read. Registry::install refuses that class, so focused tests
dispatch the descriptors directly.

help and command need the table they describe, so core_builtins binds them to
the registry passed to it through the descriptor context. That registry must
outlive the descriptors. Every other core descriptor is stateless.

Behavior notes at this level:

- echo writes its operands separated by one space and one trailing LF;
- printf supports literal text, `%s`, `%%`, and the `\\`, `\n`, `\t`, `\r`,
  `\a`, `\b`, `\f`, and `\v` escapes, reuses the format while conversions
  keep consuming operands, expands a missing operand to nothing, and refuses
  any other conversion with Status::Unsupported;
- test and `[` accept `!`, -n, -z, =, !=, -eq, -ne, -lt, -le, -gt, and -ge,
  comparing numbers through the checked 64-bit parser. `[` requires its
  closing `]`. An unknown operator or unparsable number is status 2;
- set validates every option before changing a flag, so a rejected operand
  leaves errexit and nounset exactly as they were;
- shift defaults to one, accepts a std::uint32_t decimal count, succeeds
  without change on zero, and returns 1 leaving every argument in place when
  the count exceeds `$#`;
- break and continue take no loop-count operand at this level;
- return and exit default to the current status and accept 0 through 255;
- capability lists the served capability names or queries one, with status 1
  for an unserved name and status 2 for an unknown one;
- help describes the installed table or one named command;
- command resolves a name with -v or dispatches it, reporting 127 when the
  name is not installed.

A sink that refuses bytes stops the handler at that point. WouldBlock is
reported as Status::Unavailable and a broken sink as its own failure status,
so no handler re-writes bytes it already delivered. Staged output with retry
arrives with Session.

# Platform lane qualification

The level-1 mm.shell foundation and apps/shell-smoke fixture qualification
evidence across the registered platform directories under platforms/:

- linux: host execution and native compilation (all 18 unit tests pass;
  apps/shell-smoke compiled, linked, installed to out/bin, and executed).
- arm-linux-gnueabihf: target compilation and link (apps/shell-smoke compiled
  and linked to 32-bit ARM ELF executable).
- m68k-linux-gnu: target compilation and link (apps/shell-smoke compiled and
  linked to 32-bit big-endian ELF executable via m68k-linux-gnu-g++-15).
- mps2-an385: target compilation and link (apps/shell-smoke compiled and linked
  for Cortex-M3 with semihosted specs and link.ld).
- pico: target compilation and link via external CMake bridge (apps/shell-smoke
  compiled, linked to RP2040 ELF, and valid UF2 image generated).
- pico2-arm: target compilation and link via external CMake bridge
  (apps/shell-smoke compiled, linked to RP2350 Arm Cortex-M33 ELF, and valid UF2
  image generated).
- aarch64-linux-gnu: not run: SDK sysroot /usr/aarch64-linux-gnu is not
  installed in the host environment.
- pico2-riscv: not run: riscv32-pico-elf-g++ compiler is not installed in the
  host environment.
- m68k-linux-external: recorded as an external CMake fixture rather than a
  project-module lane.
